{"context": "A research team wanted to determine whether reward hacking, in which a trained model exploits flaws in its environment to score well without doing the intended task, can emerge during reinforcement learning post-training when the training reward does not reward such behavior. Prior work had documented hacking where it was directly rewarded, but it was unclear whether it arises without that incentive. The team had a fixed compute budget and access to RL environments that differed in how easily they could be exploited. They had not yet run experiments on the unrewarded case and had no settled expectation about the outcome.", "options": ["Run smaller-scale RL post-training experiments to test whether hacking emerges without any reward for it, drawing on whatever environments the team has available for that purpose.", "Run smaller-scale RL post-training experiments in unhackable environments, such as math and hard-to-hack coding, to seek a positive result, and run more ambitious realistic pipelines to seek a negative result."], "principles": ["Prefers treating test-recognition as a first-class threat over assuming behavioural tests are reliable", "Prefers goal plus incentive inside a realistic role-play over explicit instructions to misbehave", "Prefers matched controls (incentive removed, benign intent, secure code, single-trait) over a lone headline effect", "Prefers indirect probes of eval-awareness over asking \"is this an evaluation?\"", "Prefers learning the model's own concept of eval vs deployment over researcher-chosen instances", "Prefers neutral, innocent-looking prompts and covert harm over overtly malicious content", "Prefers testing rival explanations (post-hoc blame, spurious correlates, positional bias, artifacts) before accepting a result", "Prefers out-of-distribution and held-out checks over in-distribution accuracy", "Prefers asking whether a false-belief intervention survives little or no CoT over whether it merely seems plausible"], "persona": "", "past_choices": []}
